# The store as a graph

`references` landed in 041 and made a store path more than a name. It
was still only one edge, read one way, one path at a time. This is the
rest of the shape.

## What a set of store paths costs to return

`query_all_valid_paths` answered with a `std::vector<nix::StorePath *>`
- one heap-allocated StorePath per element, owned by the binding. It
spared a re-parse, and it cost twenty lines of Cython:

    for i in range(found.size()):
        path = StorePath.__new__(StorePath)
        path._ptr = found[i]
        found[i] = NULL          # ownership handed over
        out.append(path)
    finally:
        for i in range(found.size()):
            leftover = found[i]
            if leftover is not NULL:
                del leftover     # whatever never reached a wrapper

Correct, and correct once. There are now six set-returning calls, and
a leak written into any of them is invisible until a store gets big.

Meanwhile `PathInfo.references` - added a day earlier - crossed as a
vector of base NAMES and rebuilt each StorePath in three lines. Two
patterns for one job, in one file.

So a StorePathSet crosses as its base names. `base_names` in the shim
is the mirror of `store_path_set`; `_store_paths` in the pyx is the
one place a set comes back into Python, and `references` goes through
it too.

The re-parse this costs is a 32-character check on a name the store
itself just gave. That is work nobody measures, traded for a leak
nobody can write - and it is why the five calls below are three lines
each.

## The edges

**`query_referrers`** is the inverse of `PathInfo.references`. One
says what a path points at, the other what points at it. Together they
are the edge in both directions, which is what lets a caller walk
either way - and the direction a garbage collector reads, because a
path with referrers is one something else still needs.

Only a store with a database answers. `nix::Store`'s own
implementation raises "not supported by store", the way
`query_all_valid_paths` does, because a substituter has no such index.

**`query_valid_derivers`** is NOT `PathInfo.deriver`. That one names
the .drv that actually built this path and may be gone; these are the
ones the store still holds and could build it with. `nix::Store`'s own
implementation returns an EMPTY set rather than raising, so a store
that does not track this says nothing rather than failing.

The live test asserts the difference precisely: every deriver is a
.drv, and the remembered one is among them only WHEN the store still
holds it. Unconditional would fail on any machine that has run a
collection, which is most of them.

## The closure

**`compute_fs_closure`** is what `nix-store --query --requisites`
answers, and it is the reason one edge was worth binding: `c` reaches
`a` through `b`, which reading `references` once cannot tell you. It
includes the paths it starts from, and several starting points merge
into one set rather than concatenating.

`flip_direction` walks referrers instead, so the answer is what would
BREAK if these paths went away. `include_outputs` and
`include_derivers` widen the walk at a .drv - the first follows a
derivation to what it builds, the second follows a path back to what
could build it. Both default off, which is what `nix-store -qR` does.

Sorted, because libstore answers with a set. So the order is NOT
topological, and the docstring says so rather than letting a caller
assume build order.

**`query_valid_paths`** is the set form of `is_valid_path`, and not
merely a loop over it: a store that talks to a daemon answers the
whole set in one round trip. No substitution - libstore's overload
takes a flag that would go and FETCH what is missing, which is a
different operation with a different cost and deserves its own
binding rather than a boolean hidden in this one.

## What the closure proved about the wire

It is the first call whose LIST crosses in both directions of one rpc.
`references` went out as a parameter; `query_referrers` came back as a
return; a closure does both, so the last untested combination is now
covered.

Its bool parameters ride along, and the remote test asserts the short
call and the spelled-out one mean the same thing - which is what 039
said a default has to guarantee.

## Not bound, and why

`queryDerivationOutputs` and `queryDerivationOutputMap` both need a
real .drv, and a hermetic one is not cheap: `checkDerivationOutputs`
validates that the output path matches the hash computed from the
derivation itself, so a hand-written ATerm has to be self-consistent
or the store refuses it.

The map one is worth reaching for anyway. `OutputPathMap` is
`map<string, StorePath>`, and a map of wire VALUES has machinery -
`map_to_msg` fills a message-valued entry in place - that nothing yet
exercises. `gc_stats` is a `dict[str, int]`, so only the scalar arm is
covered today.
